ll amplitude uh  perturbation so it's approximately  linear  and in the absence of any friction or  air resistance this system preserves  energy and the energy is the combination  of the  gravitational potential energy and the  kinetic energy so we know  that this system  has a conserved quantity it has uh  conserved energy  and we're going to apply dmd and  physics-informed dmd to this system  but there are two hiccups the first is  that we're going to take noisy  measurements of this system so i'm going  to measure the thetas like the angles of  the red and the orange  bars  and i'm going to add some gaussian noise  to them there's another source of noise  which is modeling noise we're modeling  this non-linear system as a linear  system so there's going to be  some unmodeled terms in our model  and the result you can see down here is  that if we train a pi dnd model in a dmd  model and try and reconstruct the data  well dmd does very poorly because its  eigenvalues end up being artificially  smaller than they should be  whereas physics informed dmd is  constrained to have eigenvalues on that  unit circle it's a it's a unitary  operator it's eigenvalues have to have  absolute value one  uh and as a result um you can see that  it doesn't let energy just go to zero  but it preserves the energy and it  obtains a really excellent  reconstruction here so the poor  performance of dmd is  very well documented in the presence of  noise and there are various algorithms  to address that and this is another  another method you could use that  includes the physical knowledge as a  regularizer that can alleviate the  effects of noise  okay  another very common physical property is  shift in variance and by shifting  variance we mean that the system we're  studying looks the same no matter how we  translate our view of the system  so such systems are common in  constant coefficient pd's and  convolutional integral equations  another way of thinking about these is  that  the operator or the system should  commute with the shift operator so the  shift operator takes in  functions and it just shifts their  arguments  if our system is shift invariant then it  should commute with that shift operator  in other words shifting the input is the  same as shifting the output  if we translate that mathematical  statement into the matrix world then  we'd see that the the matrix a that  satisfies this relation  is  circulant it has constant  diagonals with periodicity  so if we want to tackle a dmd problem  where we know that the operator should  be shifting variant then we can set up  this problem where we have the same cost  function except now a is required to  have this circular structure  this optimization problem is actually  pretty easy to solve there's just three  facts that we need to employ  the first fact is that circular matrices  are diagonalizable by dft matrices  so that means that we can write this  matrix a  in diagonal form where the the left and  right vectors are the  discrete fourier transform matrix  so this is just saying that eigenva  eigenvectors of shift invariant systems  um are just sines and cosines that's all  this relation says and then we can plug  in this representation for a and now our  task is to find this little a little a  hat which the eigenvector eigenvalues of  the system  so we've simplified the problem a bit  the next thing to notice is that the dft  matrix is unitary  and since the frobenius norm is in is  unitarily invariant  i can move this f star from this term  to this term and get rid of the star  that's just uh by hitting that whole  whole um  expression with an f  so now i've got this problem and now i  can actually break this problem into its  constituent parts by which i mean we can  decouple this frobenius norm via its  rose so the frubinius norm you often see  it as the sum of the l2 of the columns  it's also the sum of the l2 norm of the  rose and that's what we do here  and now we just have a one-dimensional  regression problem  we we've decoupled all of these ais we  just want to find an ai that best maps  f of y  uh  best maps f of x onto f of y so you can  write that solution it's just a linear  regression here i've i've written the  sort of matlab backslash notation but  you can also write that in terms of the  pseudo inverse or the at the transpose  of fx and so on  we can apply this shift and variant  principle to some pretty complicated  examples like the three-dimensional  navier-stokes equations  so in this case  we're looking at a realistic initial  condition that could be realized in  experiments and we're seeing how it  propagates with this parabolic  background flow so this is posoyflow  it's a pretty high state dimension with  about 200 000 elements in each state  factor and we only have 300 temporal  snapshots i'm only looking at this and  taking pictures of it at 300 different  points in time so it's a very  underdetermined system  we can apply dmd to this and seek a dmd  model which would necessarily be low  rank it would have to have this this  structure where we're separating a into  its modal constituents  that would be quite difficult because  the navier-stokes equations have this  convective term which is quite difficult  for dmd and these low rank  representations to capture  but we know something about this system  just by looking at it we can see that  there's a certain spatial structure  there's homogeneity in two directions  in the x and the z direction  there isn't homogeneity in the y  direction because of this um parabolic  background flow  but if we know this physics we can then  encode that using the ideas from the  previous slide into the into a pi dmd  model so now we're not just seeking a  low rank a but we're seeking an a that  respects that shift in variance so we've  done that by encoding a to have  circulant blocks  on two levels  and if we do that then you can see that  the eigenvalues learned by pi dmd are  much more accurate than those learned by  standard dmd  specifically  the least damped eigenvalues which are  really the most important ones are  obtained very accurately whereas  standard dmd doesn't really learn any of  them  okay  something else that we can include with  this physics and form perspective is  spatial locality  so spatial locality just means that  states that are spatially local to one  another that are near to one another  are more closely coupled than states  that are far apart from one another  so that's a hallmark of partial  differential equations where the the  rate of change of the state is a  function of derivatives of the state uh  it's not  a hallmark of say integral systems where  you are integrating your system over a  spatial domain  so  this is a good example of a spatially  local problem where we have a um  an advection diffusion term with a  spatially variable  advection term there  and you can see a bit how this evolves  um this is in the absence of forcing so  the f term is zero  and we're going to attack this problem  with dmd with a standard dmd method and  then our physics inform dmd but we're  going to enforce spatial locality in the  physics-informed dmd model and  specifically you can see here that means  we're going to let the  pi dmd model  only be  um tridiagonal that means the state can  only be affected by states adjacent to  it  okay  you can see what happens if we do that  the model learned by dmd has this low  rank structure the model learned by pi  emd is constrained to have this  tri-diagonal structure  um the ensuing resolvent norms and  response modes and forcing modes are  therefore much more accurate  you can see that the  response modes here  are very well captured so the yellow is  the pi dmd and the grey is the ground  truth  and also for the these resolvent norms i  should mention that that these  quantities the resolvent more norms and  these modes  are representations of um  the optimal forcing function f in the  frequency domain so if you wanted to  cause a  as large a possible perturbation to your  state c then you should choose one of  these response modes sorry one of these  forcing modes to produce a response mode  of  uh of a norm of a magnitude that  corresponds to these curves  so this is a this is resolvent analysis  specifically data-driven resolvent  analysis  another way of thinking about this is  that we're basically doing a data-driven  finite difference  method  instead of applying a finite difference  matrix we're trying to learn the best  finite difference matrix that represents  the data  okay  so here's a brief summary of all the  systems that we've studied using this  physics informed dmd  there's a few different type of physical  systems including quantum mechanics  fluid dynamics  even a neural um  a neuron equation here  there's a lot of information here so  i'll just draw your attention to these  red  columns and the point is that physics  informed dmd is much better able to  identify the eigenvalues of the system  if we tell it  something about the physics of the  underlying system  okay  so i'll wrap up here i presented this  physics informed dmd algorithm which is  a new method of seamlessly integrating  prior physical knowledge into the dmd  framework and it does so by enforcing  the learned dmd model to lie in a  specific matrix manifold  once we've calculated that that matrix  we can then look at its diagnostic  properties such as its dynamic modes or  its spectrum or its resolvent modes  through a sequence of examples we've  shown that the models learned by d by pi  dmd have greater generalization power  and they're less sensitive to noise and  they require fewer training samples and  one big reason for that is that uh we're  effectively telling dmd something about  data that it hasn't observed when we're  telling the system that it's shift  invariant or we're telling it that it's  it preserves energy we're giving it  additional information so it can  generalize more powerfully  and these pi dmd models usually have  fewer degrees of freedom than standard  dmd models so that's why they require  for your training samples additionally  we've derived what we call exact  solutions just meaning that the  solutions are in terms of standard  linear algebra objects and these are all  available on a github and the paper is  also available on archive so i invite  you to go and read it there thank you  for listening please get in touch if you  have any questions about anything
 

